{"name":"Lime Spritz","hex":"#dae1cf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any inorganic material containing calcium, usually calcium oxide (quicklime) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime).","Any gluey or adhesive substance that traps or captures; sometimes a synonym for birdlime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with calcium hydroxide or calcium oxide (lime).","To smear with birdlime.\n To ensnare, catch, entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lime"},{"term":"spritz","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sprinkling or spray of liquid; a small amount of liquid.","A small amount of something; a dash or jot."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spray, sprinkle, or squirt lightly.","To drizzle, to rain lightly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spritz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spritz (cocktail)","blocks":[{"text":"A spritz is an Italian wine-based cocktail, commonly served as an apéritif across Italy. It consists of Prosecco, a mixer, and a flavouring ingredient, which can be a bitter liqueur, a bitter apéritif, an amaro or a syrup. The original spritz veneziano uses the bitter apéritif Select as its flavouring ingredient and was created in Venice in 1920. Popular variants include the Aperol spritz, Martini vermouth spritz, Campari spritz, and Cynar spritz, which use Aperol, Campari, and Cynar as flavouring ingredients."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Aperol_Spritz_aboard_Viking_Mariella.jpg/330px-Aperol_Spritz_aboard_Viking_Mariella.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spritz_(cocktail)","wikidata":"Q668635"},{"title":"Lime (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is a color that is a shade of yellow-green, so named because it is a representation of the color of the citrus fruit lime. It is the color that is in between the web color chartreuse and yellow on the color wheel. Alternate names for this color included yellow-green, lemon-lime, lime green, or bitter lime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg/330px-AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(color)","wikidata":"Q906936"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Spritz01.jpg/500px-Spritz01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Spritz01.jpg/1280px-Spritz01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Spritz01.jpg/500px-Spritz01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Aperol spritz, served in Venice","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpritz01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Aperol_Spritz_at_Eataly_-_March_2022_-_Sarah_Stierch.jpg/500px-Aperol_Spritz_at_Eataly_-_March_2022_-_Sarah_Stierch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Aperol_Spritz_at_Eataly_-_March_2022_-_Sarah_Stierch.jpg/1280px-Aperol_Spritz_at_Eataly_-_March_2022_-_Sarah_Stierch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Aperol_Spritz_at_Eataly_-_March_2022_-_Sarah_Stierch.jpg/500px-Aperol_Spritz_at_Eataly_-_March_2022_-_Sarah_Stierch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Aperol spritz at Eataly in Las Vegas","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAperol_Spritz_at_Eataly_-_March_2022_-_Sarah_Stierch.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common